--- SYSTEMIC SWEEPS (beyond the quoted instance) ---
S1  Negated verb coordinated with "un arī" → "ne … ne" / "nedz … nedz" / rephrased.
    21 instances changed: 001 ×2, 011 ×1, 014 ×7, 016 ×2, 017 ×7, 018 ×2.
    (012 "un arī tad, kad …" in the prohibition list left as is — that is an ordinary additive clause under
    one negation, not the flagged coordination.)
S2  Debitive with an accusative object / with the dative on the wrong noun → nominative subject or an
    explicit agent. 13 instances: 001 ×2, 002 ×1, 004 ×1, 007 ×3, 010 ×1 ("Laiks … un … samazinājums
    jāpierāda", found by sweep, not quoted by either reviewer), 014 ×2, 016 ×1, 018 ×2.
S3  "pēc" carrying "depending on" with the wrong (genitive) plural → "atkarībā no" + dative. 3 instances,
    all in 002 (FAQ, body §5, and the deregistration paragraph). A file-wide "pēc" sweep was NOT done:
    the remaining ~240 occurrences are grammatical ("pēc parādu … nokārtošanas", "pēc darījumu struktūras"
    — genitive modifiers of a singular head), and replacing them wholesale is not a defect fix.
S4  "visaugstāk" (superlative adverb) as a statutory ceiling → "ne vairāk kā": 5 instances (002 ×2, 004 ×3).
S5  Term unification (R8): one Latvian rendering of 慰撫金 across 003/007/016 ("morālā kaitējuma atlīdzība");
    "trasts" spelling unified in 016; 經濟部投資審議司 already one name (001/011/013/017/018 — verified).
S6  Stock assessment verbs (lv-a systemic 6): varied only inside the sentences that were already being
    rewritten (jāizvērtē → jānoskaidro / jānovērtē / jāsadala / jāsaprot …). A file-wide thinning of the
    138 occurrences was not attempted — it would rewrite unflagged sentences wholesale and each such
    sentence still says something the source says. Flagged as remaining work for a later round.

====================================================================
§2 ROWS SKIPPED (with reason)
====================================================================
lv-a 29, 35(separator part), 56; lv-b 4, 39(separator part)
    Space thousands separators ("500 000", "1 579 589", "2 500 000", "1,57 miljoni TWD").
    SKIP per RULEBOOK R14 + ACCEPTED-ROUND: the checker only parses dot thousands for lv; a space
    separator is not parsed and the pinned bio figure "TWD 1.57M" is asserted byte-identical by a test.
    The wording halves of rows 35 and 39 WERE applied ("visaugstāk" → "ne vairāk kā", "pašu" →
    "atsevišķu"); only the digit grouping stayed.

lv-a 30, lv-b 6  — publication stamp "13. septembrī 2025. gadā" → year-first.
    TRIED AND REVERTED. `stripStructuralNoise()` in scripts/check-column-translation.mjs removes a
    line-initial "<digits>. " unless the next token is a month name, so a year-first stamp at the start of
    `date_display` loses both the year and the month. Measured:
        analyzeNumbers("2025. gada 13. septembrī","lv") -> [13]          (year + month lost)
        analyzeNumbers("13. septembrī 2025. gadā","lv") -> [13,9,2025]   (current form, correct)
        analyzeNumbers("Publicēts 2025. gada 13. septembrī","lv") -> [2025,13,9]  (parses only mid-line)
    All 18 files failed `numbers` with "missing from translation (2): 9, 2025" until the day-first form was
    restored. The in-body dates (which are mid-line) already use the correct year-first Latvian form.
    This is a checker limitation, not a Latvian decision — it needs a checker patch, not a content patch.

lv-a 31 (ZWSP part), lv-b 7  — delete the U+200B lines in 003/005/006/008/009/013/015.
    SKIP. `checkBlocks` asserts ZWSP parity with the Korean source, and the counts already match exactly:
        003 lv=52 src=52 · 005 8/8 · 006 4/4 · 008 10/10 · 009 5/5 · 013 5/5 · 015 3/3
    Deleting them would fail every one of those files ("ZWSP 52 → 0"). Turning the "Jautājums 16." body
    lines into "## 16." headings (lv-a's suggestion) would also break `checkHeadings` level parity.
    The parts of row 31 that do NOT touch ZWSP were applied (009 mid-sentence capital). The empty bold
    lines in 005 (`**​**`) are byte-identical to the Korean source at lines 38/50/60/68 — a source
    artifact, left alone for the same parity reason.

lv-a 32 (partly)  — remove the redundant digit "1".
    005 applied ("vairāk nekā vienu ārzemnieku"). 001 ("mazāk nekā 1 gadu", 4×) and 008 ("par katru
    noslēgtu 1 darba stāža gadu", 2×) TRIED AND REVERTED: the numbers bag then went short against the
    Korean source — 001 "missing from translation (4): 1×4", 008 "missing from translation (1): 1".
    In 008 exactly one of the two could be dropped without failing, but that would leave the frontmatter
    FAQ and the body blockquote saying the same thing in two different ways — the very defect lv-b 40
    reports for 017 — so both were left as they are.

lv-a 6  — drop "(10. pants)" from the 004 source-list label.
    TRIED AND REVERTED. The Korean source line 175 is "대만 법무부 법령정보 — 영업세법 제10조" and the
    numbers check counts that 10; removing it failed 004 with "missing from translation (3): 9, 10, 2025".
    The reviewer is right that the article number contradicts `flno=3` — but the contradiction is in the
    Korean source, so it is a source fix, not a Latvian one. Flagged for the supervisor.

lv-a 57  — add the Gregorian year beside the Republic-of-China year in 007.
    SKIP per ACCEPTED-ROUND (adding a Gregorian year adds a digit the checker counts). R13 is already
    satisfied: the text reads "Ķīnas Republikas 112. gada konstitucionālajā spriedumā Nr. 4
    (民國112年憲判字第4號)" — the ROC calendar is named and the gloss carries 民國.

lv-a 58 (partly) — the reviewer's replacement gloss "의료법" is Hangul; Hangul outside `url` is forbidden
    by the work order and by the checker's `hangul` lane. The row's substance was applied by dropping the
    wrong gloss instead (the English source leaves the act unglossed).

lv-b 8/9 (deletion part) — deleting two paragraphs would change the block count; rephrased inside the
    existing three blocks instead, per ACCEPTED-ROUND.

lv-b 3 (wording part) — "No 2026. gada 1. jūlija" is the natural Latvian, but the genitive month form is
    not in the checker's month table. Applied as "2026. gada 1. jūlijā un turpmāk", which carries the same
    "from that date onwards" meaning and keeps the parsable locative.

Frontmatter `url:` with Hangul in 008/009 — SKIP (ACCEPTED-ROUND; canonical source metadata).

