====================================================================
§4 THINGS I BELIEVE THE REVIEWER GOT WRONG (not applied)
====================================================================
1. lv-a 58 — the suggested gloss "(의료법)" is Hangul. The product rule and the checker both forbid Hangul
   outside `url`. The finding itself is correct (a Traditional-Chinese gloss must not be pinned to a Korean
   statute); the fix had to be "drop the gloss", not "swap it for Hangul".
2. lv-a 6 — "(10. pants)" is not a Latvian defect: the Korean source label says "영업세법 제10조" and the
   checker counts that 10. The article-number/link mismatch (and the fact that the label names a different
   act than the Latvian text does) exists in the source; only the supervisor can fix it upstream.
3. lv-a 29/30 and lv-b 4/6 — both are correct Latvian typography, but the checker cannot parse a space
   thousands separator or a year-first standalone date stamp. They are checker defects, not content
   defects; nothing in the Latvian files can satisfy both at once.
4. lv-b 7 / lv-a 31 — the U+200B lines and the empty bold lines in 005 are byte-for-byte the same as the
   Korean source and are asserted by `checkBlocks`. They cannot be removed per-locale; they have to be
   removed from the source for all 45 locales at once.
5. lv-a 32 — in 001 and 008 the digit "1" is not a Latvian addition: it is in the Korean source
   ("근속 1년", "1년 미만") and the numbers bag requires it. The stylistic point is fair, but applying it
   breaks structural parity.
6. lv-b 14 — the reviewer's replacement ("Šis slēdziens attiecas tikai uz konkrēto lietu") would have made
   the paragraph repeat the sentence two paragraphs earlier almost word for word. The English is "This
   case-specific result does not mean…", so the rewrite reads "Šis konkrētās lietas iznākums nenozīmē…".
7. lv-b 8/9 — the three 015 paragraphs are not three Latvian sentences that can be deleted: the Korean
   source has exactly those three blocks and the English is one sentence split across them. They were
   rejoined into one sentence instead of deleted.

